{
  "id": "Compound::DB00547 - Gene::2908",
  "verbalization": "Desoximetasone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the relationship with Nr3c1 through multiple pathways that converge on the therapeutic glucocorticoid class. The evidence suggests that Desoximetasone, as part of the corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist family, binds to Nr3c1, a gene encoding the glucocorticoid receptor, which is a key mediator of corticosteroid action. This binding is supported by the presence of Desoximetasone in the corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist category, which includes compounds like Desonide and Prednicarbate, all of which are known to interact with Nr3c1. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds point to the therapeutic glucocorticoid and corticosteroid hormone classes, reinforcing the biological relevance of the interaction.\n\nThe consistent evidence across the three paths indicates that Desoximetasone exerts its effects through a mechanism involving the glucocorticoid receptor, with Nr3c1 being a central target. The scores for all paths are similar, suggesting a robust and reliable relationship, and the shared pathway through the corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist class supports a unified mechanism of action. The presence of multiple compounds within the same class further strengthens the conclusion that the interaction is not limited to a single compound but is a broader pharmacological property of corticosteroids. This integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Desoximetasone and Nr3c1, mediated through the glucocorticoid receptor pathway.",
